data: {"type": "tool_end", "content": "Create chaos engineering experiments with CPU load\n\nUsage:\n  blade create k8s pod-cpu fullload\n\nAliases:\n  fullload, fl, load\n\nExamples:\n\n# Create a CPU full load experiment\nblade create k8s pod-cpu load --names nginx-app --kubeconfig ~/.kube/config --namespace default\n\n#Specifies two random kernel's full load\nblade create k8s pod-cpu load --names nginx-app --kubeconfig ~/.kube/config --namespace default --cpu-percent 60 --cpu-count 2\n\n# Specifies that the kernel is full load with index 0, 3, and that the kernel's index starts at 0\nblade create k8s pod-cpu load --names nginx-app --kubeconfig ~/.kube/config --namespace default --cpu-list 0,3\n\n# Specify the kernel full load of indexes 1-3\nblade create k8s pod-cpu load --names nginx-app --kubeconfig ~/.kube/config --namespace default --cpu-list 1-3\n\n# Specified percentage load\nblade create k8s pod-cpu load --names nginx-app --kubeconfig ~/.kube/config --namespace default --cpu-percent 60\n\nFlags:\n      --cgroup-root string               cgroup root path, default value /sys/fs/cgroup\n      --chaosblade-deploy-mode string    The mode of chaosblade deployment in container, the values are copy and download, the default value is copy which copy tool from the operator to the target container. If you select download mode, the operator will download chaosblade tool from the chaosblade-download-url.\n      --chaosblade-download-url string   The chaosblade downloaded address. If you use download deployment mode, you must specify the value, or config chaosblade-download-url when deploying the operator\n      --chaosblade-override              Override the exists chaosblade tool in the target container or not, default value is false\n      --chaosblade-path string           Chaosblade tool deployment path, default value is /opt. Please select a path with write permission\n      --climb-time string                durations(s) to climb\n      --cpu-count string                 Cpu count\n      --cpu-index string                 cpu index, user unavailable!\n      --cpu-list string                  CPUs in which to allow burning (0-3 or 1,3)\n      --cpu-percent string               percent of burn CPU (0-100)\n      --evict-count string               Count of affected resource\n      --evict-group string               Group key from labels\n      --evict-percent string             Percent of affected resource, integer value without %\n  -h, --help                             help for fullload\n      --labels string                    Label selector, the relationship between values that are or\n      --names string                     Resource names, such as pod name. You must add namespace flag for it. Multiple parameters are separated directly by commas\n      --namespace string                 Namespace, such as default, only one value can be specified (required)\n      --timeout string                   set timeout for experiment\n\nGlobal Flags:\n  -a, --async                  whether to create asynchronously, default is false\n      --cluster-uuid string    Target cluster UUID in kubewiz (required when using --kubewiz-url)\n  -d, --debug                  Set client to DEBUG mode\n  -e, --endpoint string        the create result reporting address. It takes effect only when the async value is true and the value is not empty\n      --kubeconfig string      kubeconfig file\n      --kubectl-proxy string   Kubectl proxy URL for accessing Kubernetes API, e.g., http://localhost:8001\n      --kubewiz-token string   JWT token for kubewiz-core authentication\n      --kubewiz-url string     Kubewiz core service URL for delegated K8s operations, e.g., http://kubewiz-core:8080\n  -n, --nohup                  used to internal async create, no need to config\n      --token string           Bearer token for Kubernetes API authentication\n      --uid string             Set Uid for the experiment, adapt to docker and cri\n      --waiting-time string    Waiting time for invoking, default value is 20s", "node": "phase1_tools", "tool_name": "read_file", "task_id": "turn-e708412252a4", "call_id": "01a08fa6-c578-7ae3-8679-0292ff119d96", "timestamp": "2026-09-11T16:47:46.575022+08:00"}

data: {"type": "tool_end", "content": "Cpu experiment, for example full load\n\nUsage:\n  blade create k8s pod-cpu [flags]\n  blade create k8s pod-cpu [command]\n\nAvailable Commands:\n  fullload    cpu load\n\nFlags:\n  -h, --help   help for pod-cpu\n\nGlobal Flags:\n  -a, --async                  whether to create asynchronously, default is false\n      --cluster-uuid string    Target cluster UUID in kubewiz (required when using --kubewiz-url)\n  -d, --debug                  Set client to DEBUG mode\n  -e, --endpoint string        the create result reporting address. It takes effect only when the async value is true and the value is not empty\n      --kubeconfig string      kubeconfig file\n      --kubectl-proxy string   Kubectl proxy URL for accessing Kubernetes API, e.g., http://localhost:8001\n      --kubewiz-token string   JWT token for kubewiz-core authentication\n      --kubewiz-url string     Kubewiz core service URL for delegated K8s operations, e.g., http://kubewiz-core:8080\n  -n, --nohup                  used to internal async create, no need to config\n      --token string           Bearer token for Kubernetes API authentication\n      --uid string             Set Uid for the experiment, adapt to docker and cri\n      --waiting-time string    Waiting time for invoking, default value is 20s\n\nUse \"blade create k8s pod-cpu [command] --help\" for more information about a command.", "node": "phase1_tools", "tool_name": "blade_help", "task_id": "turn-e708412252a4", "call_id": "01a08fa6-c577-7342-92d0-daf09d1390e2", "timestamp": "2026-09-11T16:47:47.625454+08:00"}

